3. Why was ZFS developed?

Older filesystems had limitations.

Companies wanted:

Easy storage management
Better performance
Data protection
Snapshots
Compression

Oracle introduced ZFS to provide all these features.

4. Main Features of ZFS
a) Storage Pool

ZFS combines disks into one storage pool.

Example:

You have

Disk1 = 500 GB

Disk2 = 500 GB

Create a pool.

zpool create datapool c1t0d0 c1t1d0

Now,

Storage Pool

↓

1000 GB
b) Filesystem Creation

Create a filesystem.

zfs create datapool/projects

Now users can store project files.

c) Snapshots

Suppose your database contains customer records.

Before patching,

Create a snapshot.

zfs snapshot datapool/projects@before_patch

If the patch fails,

Restore the snapshot.

This is similar to LVM Snapshots, but ZFS snapshots are generally more efficient and easier to manage.

d) Compression

Suppose you have

100 GB of text files.

Enable compression.

zfs set compression=on datapool/projects

Now the files may occupy much less space (the exact savings depend on the data).

e) Data Integrity

ZFS automatically checks whether data is corrupted.

If corruption is detected and redundant copies exist (for example, with mirrors or RAID-Z), ZFS can repair the data automatically.

This is one of ZFS's biggest advantages.

f) Dynamic Expansion

Suppose your pool is

500 GB

Later,

add another disk.

Now,

1000 GB

No need to recreate everything.

5. Common ZFS Commands
a);Create Storage Pool
zpool create datapool c1t0d0

Creates a pool.

b) View Pools
zpool list

Example:

NAME

datapool

c) Check Pool Health
zpool status

Shows whether disks are healthy.

Example:

pool: datapool

state: ONLINE

d) List Filesystems
zfs list

Example:

NAME

datapool

datapool/projects

e) List Snapshots
zfs list -t snapshot

f) Destroy Snapshot
zfs destroy datapool/projects@backup1

Deletes the snapshot.
